James sought help after accidentally committing a large file to a Git repository and needing to remove it. I guided him through using `git filter-repo` to delete the file from his history, emphasizing the importance of backing up the repository and informing collaborators after a force push. He then inquired about finding files in his last commit, for which I provided commands like `git show --name-only` and `git diff-tree`. After using `filter-repo`, James faced an error indicating that the `origin` remote no longer existed. I advised him to verify remotes with `git remote -v`, and then to re-add the remote using `git remote add origin <repository-url>`, followed by instructions on pushing changes.
